## opensift-container_images

standard ubi images
- universal base image (size/usablility treshold), predefined types: standard/init/minimal/micro
- source-to-image deployment scripts -- ubi images contain those, so that you can use them as builders for your application
- should be tested by RH
- runtime images -- versioned popular runtimes on ubi

# optimizing containers for os

- use rh runtime images, e.g.:
FROM registry.access.redhat.com/ubi9/nodejs-18-minimal:1-56
- check the UBI specific instructions in the RH ecosystem catalog 
- os by default ignores USER instructions in dockerfiles and uses a random user that is a member of a root group
- so for the contaier instructions keep in mind: root group must have perms to read/write/execute any dirs & files, use: 
RUN chgrp -R 0 directory && chmod -R g=u directory
- container should handle interruption signals, os sends SITERM to containers (use entrypoit, or preStop lifecycle hook)
- use container footprint reduction techniques
- use metadata labels

# pushing to quay registry

podman images
podman push localhost/myapache quay.io/user/image_name

- using private image with oc
- oc create-app with image will fail if there is no access configured
- opensift needs to be able to authenticate to quay with the os credentials:

# 'secret' resource
- not easily revealed in cli, ui
- similar resource config-map -- can be printed, not so with secrets
- os can encrypt secret if configured to do so
- 3 types: tls, generic, docker-registry

oc create secret docker-registry quay --docker-username=<usr> --docker-password=<pwd> --docker-server=quay.io

# 'service-account'
- binds secrets and other metadata to service
- types: builder, default, builder, pipeline -- service accounts to match with deployments...

oc get sa
oc describe sa default

- binding a secret to default would make it available to all user

oc create sa quayaccess
- gets some defualt secret type: pull, mountable, tokens

oc secrets link service-account-name secret-name --for=pull
- binds secret to service account, for a specific reason

oc explain deployment.spec.template.spec|less
- just to check wich deployment param is being set (ServiceAccountName)

oc set serviceaccount deployment/myappache <sa-name>
- to set for an existing deployment
- or edit the spec for deployment under ImagePullSecrests

# imagestream resource
- new-app creates it

oc import-image <is-name>:<tag> --from registry:8443/image:tag -f
- create imagestream during importing an image
oc describe is <is-name>
- images stream is a container for imagestream tags
- is gives me control over versioning

oc new-app --name myapache myweb:latest 
- changing the version to a custom one
oc tag myweb:2 myweb:latest

- image is not directly reflected in deployment, it is done trough the imagestream, so it is a kind of intermediate resource 

oc tag --alias myweb:3 myweb:latest 
- creates a link between tags
- without --alias, I would be creating a copy

